# Nested worktrees created inside the repo by mistake. Worktrees belong in
# ../.worktrees/, and a stray one here would otherwise be committed as content.
/.worktrees/

# Agent-state CACHE. directive.md and decisions.ndjson are the durable, tracked
# state; everything here is re-derivable and machine-local. The stop-hook
# fingerprint records a local HEAD sha and open-item count, so tracking it made
# every branch churn the same line (44 commits) and conflict with every other
# branch for no shared information.
.agent-state/.last-stop-fingerprint
.agent-state/.last-stop-sha
.agent-state/.last-compaction
.agent-state/.indexed-sha
.agent-state/digest.md
.agent-state/cursor.md
.agent-state/batch.json

# Ralph runtime state is user-level (XDG state root), never repo-committed.
# AGENTS.md: "There is NO committed repo state -- no .radioactive-ralph/ dir."
.radioactive-ralph/

# Per-turn provider result files. internal/provider/codex.go creates these in
# the project dir and removes them on cleanup, but a self-test run in flight
# leaves them present -- and `git add -A` then commits runtime state into a
# repo whose contract forbids exactly that.
.ralph-codex-result-*.txt

# Provider scratch dirs. A contained codex turn sets HOME/TMPDIR under the
# project so its writes stay inside the containment root, which means a
# self-test in flight leaves these here -- and their contents churn fast enough
# that `git add -A` can fail mid-stat on a file the turn already deleted.
.codex-tmp/
.codex-*-go/
